# Review ownership. The last matching pattern wins, so order matters.
#
# Keep a real human on `*` — an unowned repository has no reviewer, and a
# ruleset that requires a CODEOWNERS review will block every pull request.

# Default owner for everything in the repository.
*                       @fschmutz

# Repository configuration: workflows, ownership, and issue templates change the
# rules everyone else works under, so they are reviewed separately.
/.github/               @fschmutz
/.github/workflows/     @fschmutz
/.github/CODEOWNERS     @fschmutz

# Security posture and licensing.
/SECURITY.md            @fschmutz
/LICENSE                @fschmutz

# The contract every AI assistant works under, and the bundles that are
# deliberately switched off. Both change what runs without changing what the
# repository does, which is exactly the kind of diff that gets skimmed.
/AGENTS.md              @fschmutz
/CLAUDE.md              @fschmutz
/.github/copilot-instructions.md @fschmutz
/.github/bundles/       @fschmutz

# Secret-scanning and hook configuration — relaxing these is a security decision.
/.github/gitleaks.toml  @fschmutz
/.pre-commit-config.yaml @fschmutz

# The supply-chain gate and its declared exceptions: a hold here is a decision
# to stay behind a release, so it is reviewed like one.
/.dependency-holds.json @fschmutz
/scripts/check-latest.mjs @fschmutz

# Add path owners as the repository grows, for example:
# /site/pacman/         @fschmutz @deputy
